from sampler import sample_users

def test_sample_is_diverse():
    users = list(range(100))
    picks = sample_users(users, 10)
    assert len(set(picks)) == 10
    assert picks != sorted(picks)

The second assertion fails whenever the random sample happens to come out ascending, roughly 1 in 3.6 million runs per test, but ALSO the team saw it fail weekly because CI runs it thousands of times; and it asserts something that is not part of the contract anyway. Your job: (1) demonstrate the failure deterministically by finding a seed where random.sample returns an ascending result for these parameters (search seeds in a loop until you find one, print it, and pin it in a test showing the old assertion breaks); (2) rewrite the tests properly: inject a random.Random instance into sample_users (default random) so tests pass rng=random.Random(42) and assert exact expected output, plus contract tests (no duplicates, all picks from the population, k >= len returns a copy not the same object, and mutating the copy leaves the original intact); (3) a statistical test done RIGHT: with a seeded rng, 1000 samples of k=1 from 4 users hits every user at least once. pytest green 30 runs in a row.

